+    // THE INHERITANCE CLAUSE — PostgreSQL's table inheritance, in BOTH of the forms the language has:
+    //
+    //   CREATE TABLE child ( ... ) INHERITS ( parent [, ...] )   — the parent LIST
+    //   ALTER TABLE child INHERIT parent                         — ONE parent
+    //   ALTER TABLE child NO INHERIT parent                      — the REMOVAL of one parent
+    //
+    // WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS. Before it the grammar had NO production for any of the three, so
+    // `INHERITS` / `INHERIT` were lexed as plain identifiers and the whole clause ERROR-recovered:
+    // the parent name did not survive the parse. That is why the analyzer could publish no `extends`
+    // edge for the CREATE TABLE form at all, and why it had to read the ALTER TABLE form out of a
+    // MIS-PARSE — the clause arrives as a `column_definition` whose name is literally `INHERIT` and
+    // whose type is the parent table. The construct EXISTS in the language and was only PARTIALLY
+    // readable in the grammar; this patch is the R2 rung that makes it readable AS A REAL NODE.
+    //
+    // THE SHAPE, AND WHY IT IS THIS SHAPE. `inherit_clause` holds the parent LIST as
+    // `object_reference` children, and `inherit_specification` holds ONE parent, with `keyword_no`
+    // marking the removal form. Both keep the parent's own name in a node the analyzer can read, so
+    // the edge exists only when the construct REACHES the analyzer as a real node — never as
+    // recovery text. `prec(1)` is on `inherit_specification` because `ALTER TABLE c INHERIT p` is ALSO
+    // readable as `add_column` (a column named `INHERIT` of type `p`), and the inheritance reading
+    // must win: the other reading is the mis-parse this patch exists to remove.
+    inherit_clause: $ => seq(
+      $.keyword_inherits,
+      "(",
+      $.object_reference,
+      repeat(seq(",", $.object_reference)),
+      ")",
+    ),
+
+    inherit_specification: $ => prec(1, seq(
+      optional($.keyword_no),
+      $.keyword_inherit,
+      $.object_reference,
+    )),
+
     _alter_specifications: $ => choice(
+      $.inherit_specification,
       $.add_column,
       $.add_constraint,
       $.drop_constraint,
